WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.354 --> 00:00:02.937
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.590
- [Narrator] This is
what Facebook's offices

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.680
normally look like, expansive spaces

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.450
with plenty of areas to meet,

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:14.160
and rows of desks, open office style,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.410
something CEO Mark Zuckerberg showed off

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.270
after the new Silicon Valley
headquarters opened in 2015.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.720
- We've worked like this for a long time

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.940
and it's been a pretty core way

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.333
for how we do what we do here at Facebook.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.910
- [Narrator] Facebook's
new New York offices

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.010
should look just as bustling.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.300
Instead, they're full of empty chairs

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.953
and vacant conference rooms.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.200
The only workers around are the ones

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.100
building out the company's
more than 2 million square feet

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.853
of leased office space on
the West side of Manhattan.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.203
But employees have yet to move in.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.900
- I think that it's quite possible

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.650
that over the next five to 10 years

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.570
about 50% of our people
could be working remotely.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.150
- [Narrator] Facebook
shifted to remote work

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.390
early in the pandemic,

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.440
and was one of the first to lay out a plan

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.350
for some employees to work
from home indefinitely.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:07.720
- We are seeing that productivity

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.350
has actually been really
solid working remotely,

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.730
but I think there, no matter what,

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.940
is going to be some type of future,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:15.970
where we are seeing the value

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.670
in both remote and in office work.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.490
- [Narrator] Even as
Facebook has been preparing

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.140
for a more flexible future,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.860
it has been expanding
physical space in New York,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.010
signing a major lease during the pandemic

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.813
to become one of the city's
largest corporate tenants.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.790
So why is Facebook doubling down

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.780
on in-person work in New York?

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.870
And what impact could its growing presence

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.853
have on the future of tech in the city?

00:01:41.676 --> 00:01:42.509
(playful music)

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:44.980
Facebook first came to New York in 2007,

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.480
opening its office with a handful

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.500
of people focused on
marketing and advertising.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.880
Becca Foy, who now heads
Facebook's business teams

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.150
in New York was one of the
office's earliest employees.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.070
- I joined the company when we were less

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.530
than 500 people globally,

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.220
less than 20 or so here in New York.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.320
And now we are over 4,000 employees,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.740
half of which are tech and engineering.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.100
- [Narrator] Then in 2019, Facebook signed

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.500
a lease in Hudson Yards,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.310
a new development in the city.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.140
That deal roughly tripled Facebook's

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.503
square footage in New York.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.880
In 2020, in the midst of the pandemic,

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.780
Facebook again expanded
its footprint in the city,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.710
adding an additional
730,000 square feet of space

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.990
in the former Farley Post Office building,

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.721
the largest single lease
signed in Manhattan last year.

00:02:32.721 --> 00:02:34.260
- The companies go where the people

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.593
they want to hire are.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.470
Facebook doubling down in New York

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.207
sends a really loud message

00:02:41.207 --> 00:02:44.540
that the people they want
to hire are in New York.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.540
- [Narrator] Located just down
the street from Hudson Yards,

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.210
the space at Farley Post
gives Facebook something

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.080
that's a little bit more Silicon Valley

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.590
than New York City, a kind of campus.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.240
- As we think about this urban campus

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.090
and really growing the
footprint is absolutely vital

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.550
to the future for a few reasons.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:01.746
Not only do we need to accommodate

00:03:01.746 --> 00:03:04.190
our growing number of employees,

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:07.140
but really accommodate the
needs and the preferences

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.290
of how they're gonna
work most effectively.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.700
- [Narrator] The completed spaces

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.200
look like many of
Facebook's other offices,

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.650
with open office seating

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.550
and a variety of meeting rooms,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.580
and for the floor is
still under construction.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.640
- You can imagine spaces
that are right for events,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.580
having collaboration areas,

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.250
with creative agencies, et cetera.

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.310
And so our business scenes
are really gonna look

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.353
to Hudson Yards as their hub
as we reopen those offices.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.010
And then on the flip side,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.513
the Farley Post building,
that is really gonna become

00:03:37.513 --> 00:03:39.230
more of a dedicated tech hub

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.480
for our tech and engineering teams.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.470
- [Narrator] Despite the pandemic,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.530
Foy says Facebook didn't make any major

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.080
structural changes to its office designs.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.810
Facebook doesn't plan to reopen

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.970
until it sees it as safe to do so.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.400
But that's a matter of when, not if.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.840
- When we do open our offices,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.000
the health and safety piece

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.600
is going to be our number one priority.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.460
And so if you imagine
walking into the offices,

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.700
I can't predict exactly
what that will look like,

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.560
but I know that we will
take those protocols,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.910
and just the experience of coming

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:08.823
into the building really seriously.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.779
- There's been plenty of beans spilled

00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:15.550
in all kinds of newspapers and magazines

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.400
about how people are just
leaving New York City in droves.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.172
But actually we're not
really seeing that in tech.

00:04:20.172 --> 00:04:23.570
(upbeat music)

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.590
- [Narrator] Julie Samuels
is the executive director

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.020
at Tech:NYC, a coalition
of about 800 tech companies

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.740
and investors working on behalf

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.770
of the industry at the
city and state level.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.510
She says the industry has
fared well during the pandemic

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.660
as it was already more set
up for working from home.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.030
She doesn't think that shift

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.080
will push firms out of New York.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.530
- I think that the tech sector

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.990
is fundamentally committed to the city.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.550
I think, however, the tech sector,

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.260
generally speaking, is much
more kind of open-minded

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.850
about where it works and how it works.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:58.960
- [Narrator] Those smaller tech firms

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.010
aren't taking direction from Facebook.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.530
The major leases it and
other big tech firms

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.620
signed in 2020 could influence
tech's future in the city.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:08.860
- What's gonna happen is people

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.300
are gonna go work at Facebook.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.310
They're gonna learn about tech,

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.880
and honestly, I mean, I love Facebook,

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.410
but I hope a bunch of them leave

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.160
and I hope that they
start their own companies.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.413
Because that, that's the magic.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.750
- [Narrator] For Facebook,
when exactly workers

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.070
will return to work is unclear.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.230
But Foy says a majority of employees

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.270
have expressed the desire to come back.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:36.160
- Mark Zuckerberg will
say some of our products

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.520
and things that we're building,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.660
they can happen in a matter of months.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.380
But building a culture happens day by day,

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.970
over years, over decades,

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.460
and having our offices
and our physical locations

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.800
are gonna continue to
be such a contributor

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:49.853
to our culture.

